@database "script_interpreter"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: script_interpreter.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Script Interpreter"
@{h1}Script Interpreter

Language models are unreliable at arithmetic and at counting. With this
plugin an agent computes instead of guessing: it sends a few lines of
Python -- sum a column of figures, work out a percentage, count words, sort
a list -- and gets back what they printed. The code runs in a sandbox
inside the server: a subset of Python that cannot reach files, the network
or the server itself. What a script assigns stays there for the next one,
so an agent can work on a result step by step.

The plugin has no panel. You see a run in the chat's status line: how much
it printed, how many variables the session holds, and how long it took.

The same sandbox runs the scripts of the @{" tool_script " link tool_script/main}
plugin; there a script can also call the agent's tools.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  The tools  " link tool}
  @{"  The script language  " link language}
  @{"  Limits  " link limits}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tool "The tools"
@{h1}The tools

The plugin gives an agent two tools, named after the plugin instance:
@{tt}script_interpreter_execute@{utt} and
@{tt}script_interpreter_reset@{utt} in the default setup.

@{h2}One sandbox per session

Each chat session has a sandbox of its own. Variables, and functions made
with @{tt}def@{utt}, stay in it from one call to the next until the agent
resets it; another session never sees them. A sub-agent runs in a session
of its own, and so in a sandbox of its own. A call without a session shares
one sandbox, "default", with every other such call.

The server keeps at most 100 sandboxes; one that has not been used for an
hour, or the least recently used beyond the 100, is dropped when another
session's call comes in -- its variables are gone, and the next call starts
empty. Two calls in the same session run one after the other.

@{h2}execute

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
code | the Python source; required, an empty one answers @{tt}{"error": "Missing required parameter 'code'"}@{utt}
@{body}

The code runs statement by statement. What it prints is the output. Only
code that is one lone expression is shown without @{tt}print@{utt}:
@{tt}2 + 3@{utt} answers 5, but @{tt}x = 5@{utt} followed by
@{tt}x * 2@{utt} prints nothing.

A run that ended normally answers one text:

@{code json}
{"result": "Output: 41.75\nVariables: prices=[12.5, 8.0, 21.25], total=41.75\nExecution time: 0.001s"}
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}Output:@{utt} what was printed; missing when nothing was.
@{bullet}@{tt}Variables:@{utt} every variable of the session, not only the
ones this run assigned. A value up to 200 characters is shown as it is;
a longer one only as type and size (@{tt}notes=<str, 500 chars —
omitted>@{utt}), a lambda as @{tt}<function>@{utt}. Functions made with
@{tt}def@{utt} are not listed.
@{bullet}@{tt}Execution time:@{utt} in seconds.

A run that failed:

@{code json}
{"error": {"type": "ZeroDivisionError", "message": "float division by zero", "category": "unknown", "line_number": 2, "problematic_line": "share = total / (len(prices) - 3)", "code_context": "    1: print('checking')\n>>> 2: share = total / (len(prices) - 3)"}, "error_message": "Runtime error on line 2: float division by zero\n\nCode context:\n    1: print('checking')\n>>> 2: share = total / (len(prices) - 3)", "output": "checking"}
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}error@{utt}: the exception's type and message, a category
(@{tt}syntax@{utt}, @{tt}name_error@{utt}, @{tt}unsupported_feature@{utt},
often @{tt}unknown@{utt}), the script line -- also inside a loop or a
function --, that line and up to two lines around it, and for some errors a
@{tt}suggestion@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}error_message@{utt}: the same as one text, "Runtime error on
line N: ..." or "Syntax error on line N: ...", with the code context.
@{bullet}@{tt}output@{utt}: what the script printed before the error, when
it printed.

Nothing is rolled back: what the lines before the error assigned stays in
the session. A syntax error runs nothing. Something the sandbox does not
support -- @{tt}import@{utt}, @{tt}break@{utt}, @{tt}del@{utt} -- fails
with the type @{tt}UnsupportedFeatureError@{utt} and a message such as
"Import statements are not allowed in sandbox: import math" or "Unsupported
AST node type: Break".

@{h2}reset

No parameters. Clears the session's sandbox: variables and functions. It
always answers @{tt}{"result": "🔄 Python sandbox reset - all variables and
state cleared"}@{utt}, also when the session had no sandbox yet.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"code": "prices = [12.5, 8.0, 21.25]\ntotal = sum(prices)\nprint(f'{total:.2f}')"}
{"code": "print(round(total / len(prices), 2))"}
@{body}

The second call uses @{tt}prices@{utt} and @{tt}total@{utt} from the
first.
@endnode

@node language "The script language"
@{h1}The script language

Scripts are a subset of Python, run by an interpreter that walks the
syntax tree and allows only what it knows. There is no way to reach a file,
the network or the server's own objects: no import, no
@{tt}open@{utt}, @{tt}eval@{utt} or @{tt}exec@{utt}, and attributes only on
text, lists, dicts and sets (their listed methods) and a type's
@{tt}__name__@{utt} (@{tt}type(x).__name__@{utt}) -- anything else,
@{tt}"".__class__@{utt} for instance, is refused.

@{h2}What works

@{bullet}Assignments, also @{tt}a, *rest = items@{utt} and
@{tt}+=@{utt}; @{tt}if@{utt}/@{tt}elif@{utt}/@{tt}else@{utt}, @{tt}for@{utt},
@{tt}while@{utt}, @{tt}x if c else y@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}def@{utt} with positional parameters and defaults,
@{tt}return@{utt}, recursion; @{tt}lambda@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}try@{utt}/@{tt}except@{utt}/@{tt}else@{utt}/@{tt}finally@{utt},
@{tt}raise ValueError("...")@{utt}.
@{bullet}Lists, dicts, sets, tuples; list, dict and set comprehensions and
generator expressions; slicing; f-strings with format specs
(@{tt}f"{x:.2f}"@{utt}); @{tt}%@{utt} formatting; @{tt}in@{utt},
@{tt}is@{utt}, chained comparisons.
@{bullet}Methods: text -- @{tt}split join replace strip lstrip rstrip upper
lower find rfind index rindex count startswith endswith partition
rpartition splitlines center ljust rjust zfill title capitalize swapcase
casefold@{utt} and the @{tt}is...@{utt} tests; lists -- @{tt}append extend
insert remove pop clear count index reverse sort copy@{utt}; dicts --
@{tt}get items keys values pop popitem update setdefault clear copy@{utt};
sets -- @{tt}add remove discard pop clear copy update union intersection
difference symmetric_difference issubset issuperset isdisjoint@{utt}.
@{bullet}Functions: @{tt}len range sorted enumerate min max sum abs round
int float str bool list dict set tuple type isinstance print format hex
bin oct chr ord@{utt}, math (@{tt}sqrt floor ceil pow log log10 exp sin cos
tan asin acos atan sinh cosh tanh degrees radians@{utt}) and @{tt}mean
median mode stdev@{utt}. @{tt}min@{utt} and @{tt}max@{utt} take
@{tt}key=@{utt} (a @{tt}lambda@{utt}) and @{tt}default=@{utt}.

@{h2}What does not

@{bullet}@{tt}import@{utt} -- nothing can be imported, not even math; the
math functions above are there without it.
@{bullet}@{tt}break@{utt} and @{tt}continue@{utt}: use a flag, or a
function that returns.
@{bullet}@{tt}del@{utt}, @{tt}with@{utt}, @{tt}class@{utt},
@{tt}assert@{utt}, @{tt}global@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}zip any all map filter reversed divmod@{utt},
@{tt}str.format@{utt} (use f-strings), @{tt}str.encode@{utt}.
@{bullet}Names starting with @{tt}_@{utt} (@{tt}_@{utt} alone is allowed),
and the bare name @{tt}__name__@{utt}: call a function directly instead
of @{tt}if __name__ == "__main__":@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}while True:@{utt} is refused as written; any other loop is
bounded (see @{" Limits " link limits}).

@{h2}Surprises

@{bullet}@{tt}pi@{utt} and @{tt}e@{utt} are functions: @{tt}pi()@{utt},
@{tt}e()@{utt}. @{tt}pow(2, 10)@{utt} gives the float 1024.0; with a third
argument it stays an integer.
@{bullet}Your own functions take positional arguments only:
@{tt}f(1, b=5)@{utt} ignores @{tt}b@{utt} and uses its default.
@{bullet}A function reads the session's variables, but names it assigns are
gone when it returns; a list or dict it changes stays changed.
@{bullet}One exception type per @{tt}except@{utt}: @{tt}except (KeyError,
ValueError):@{utt} catches neither. @{tt}except ValueError@{utt} also
catches RuntimeError.
@{bullet}@{tt}sum()@{utt} adds numbers only: for lists use
@{tt}extend@{utt}, for text @{tt}join@{utt}.
@{bullet}A list or dict that contains itself: @{tt}print(a)@{utt} shows
@{tt}[...]@{utt} where it repeats, but @{tt}str(a)@{utt} and
@{tt}f"{a}"@{utt} are refused as too large.
@endnode

@node limits "Limits"
@{h1}Limits

@{table}
Limit | Default | What happens
max_execution_time | 5 s | the whole run. An except may catch the TimeoutError, but the next step raises it again, so the run ends with "Execution exceeded 5.0 seconds"
loop_timeout_seconds | 2 s | one loop, counted from its start, nested loops included: "Loop exceeded 2.0 seconds". A script can catch that one and go on -- but not inside an enclosing loop, whose own next check raises it too
max_loop_iterations | 100000 | passes of one loop, and the numbers one @{tt}range()@{utt} may give; past it the loop fails with an error a script can catch
max_output_length | 10000 | characters of @{tt}print@{utt} output in one run; past it the run ends ("Output exceeds maximum allowed length"), and no except catches that
max_memory_mb | 50 | not a memory limit: it sets how long a text or list may grow, 131072 items per MB -- 6553600 at the default
@{body}

Fixed in the sandbox:

@{bullet}Arithmetic that would take the server more than milliseconds is
refused at once: numbers over 200000 bits (about 60000 digits) in
@{tt}**@{utt}, @{tt}*@{utt}, @{tt}//@{utt} and @{tt}%@{utt}; a
three-argument @{tt}pow@{utt} with an exponent or modulus over 2048 bits;
sorting more than a million items; @{tt}round(n, -d)@{utt} with
@{tt}d@{utt} over 60000; @{tt}int(text, base)@{utt} over 200000 bits;
@{tt}sum min max set sorted mean median mode stdev@{utt} over about 30
million characters of content -- a thousand copies of a 60000-digit number,
say; plain floats and small numbers never reach it.
@{bullet}A text or list may not grow past the length cap above -- by
@{tt}*@{utt}, @{tt}+@{utt}, @{tt}extend@{utt}, @{tt}ljust@{utt},
@{tt}rjust@{utt}, @{tt}center@{utt}, @{tt}zfill@{utt}, @{tt}replace@{utt},
@{tt}join@{utt}, a format or @{tt}%@{utt} width or precision
(@{tt}f"{x:>200000000}"@{utt}, @{tt}"%*d" % (n, 1)@{utt}), or turning a
big container into text (@{tt}str@{utt}, @{tt}print@{utt}, f-strings,
@{tt}format@{utt}, @{tt}%@{utt}, an error message) -- the size is estimated
before the text is built.
@{bullet}A function can call itself a few hundred levels deep (roughly 200
to 300, depending on the function); deeper ends in a RecursionError.
@{bullet}A memory fuse: when the server process grows by more than 1 GB
while the script runs, the script ends ("Memory grew by more than 1024 MB
during the script; aborted"), and no except catches that. It is read every
50 ms.

A cancel of the agent's run is checked once, before the script starts
(@{tt}{"error": "Python execution cancelled by user", "cancelled":
true}@{utt}); a script already running goes on to its end or its time
limit.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The tool descriptions come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}: what
the sandbox supports, no imports and no @{tt}break@{utt}/@{tt}continue@{utt},
that variables and functions stay until a reset, and that a value needs
@{tt}print()@{utt}. The error answer is made for the model to correct the
next call from: the line, the code around it, what was printed before.

In the chat, the status line shows "Python execution started" and ends
with "Executed: 5 chars output, 2 variable(s), 0.001s", or "Execution
failed: Runtime error on line 2: ..." (cut at 120 characters). A reset
ends with "Sandbox reset for session <id>".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Each answer is an ordinary tool result: appended, nothing earlier changes,
the cached prompt stays as it was. Every successful answer lists all the
session's variables again -- a value over 200 characters replaced by
@{tt}<type, N chars — omitted>@{utt}: a session that
keeps many values pays for them on every call -- reset when a task is
done. Output is capped at 10000 characters; an error answer is its
message, a few lines of the script, and what was printed.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The memory fuse measures the whole server process: other work
growing at the same time counts against the script, and a loop that
allocates fast can pass the 1 GB by a few hundred MB before the next
reading. Without psutil there is no fuse.
@{bullet}A running script cannot be cancelled; it holds a worker thread up
to its time limit.
@{bullet}Functions made with @{tt}def@{utt} are kept but never listed; the
agent has to remember them.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    script_interpreter:
      type: script_interpreter
      enabled: true
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+script_interpreter/*"
@{body}

The plugin needs nothing beyond the framework; psutil, which the memory
fuse reads, is one of its core dependencies. It keeps no data on disk; the sandboxes live in the server's
memory and are gone when it restarts.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings go in a @{tt}script_interpreter:@{utt} block inside the
server entry:

@{code yaml}
    script_interpreter:
      type: script_interpreter
      enabled: true
      script_interpreter:
        max_execution_time: 10
        max_output_length: 20000
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_execution_time | 5.0 | seconds for one run
loop_timeout_seconds | 2.0 | seconds for one loop
max_loop_iterations | 100000 | passes of one loop, and the largest range()
max_output_length | 10000 | characters of print output per run
max_memory_mb | 50 | sets the length cap of texts and lists, 131072 items per MB
allowed_functions | the functions in @{" The script language " link language} | replaces the list; it can only narrow it, and int, str, float, bool, list, dict, tuple and set always work
enable_variables | true | false refuses plain assignments (@{tt}x = ...@{utt})
session_ttl_seconds | 3600 | seconds an unused sandbox is kept; 0 keeps it until the limit below
max_tracked_sessions | 100 | sandboxes kept; beyond it the least recently used is dropped
@{body}

Keys the plugin does not know are ignored.
@endnode
